// $Module type: Class Package
// $Module name: cWorkspaceCopyPlan
// $Author     : Nils Svedmyr, RDC Tools International, <mailto:support@rdctools.com>
// Web-site    : http://www.rdctools.com
// Created     : 2026-08-05
//
// Description : Builds the migration copy plan: which files a workspace copy carries, and
//               which stay behind. The scope rules live here so the copy step and its tests
//               share one authority.
//
// This code is distributed in the hope that it will be useful, but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY;
// without even the implied warranty of MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.
//****************************************************************************
Use RefactorConstants.h.pkg
Use RefactorFileIO.pkg
Use cLibraryInventory.pkg
Use cEncodingHelpers.pkg         // rfHtmlEscape and the encoding helpers

/*  cWorkspaceCopyPlan - what does "this workspace" actually consist of, and what would copying it
    cost?

    Step 0 of a DataFlex-version migration. Copying the workspace first is what lets the migration
    be a DERIVATION instead of an in-place mutation: the original keeps compiling and running while
    the copy gets the new .sws, the retargeted libraries and the refactoring. Every other safety
    mechanism in DFRefactor (the backup tree, Undo Refactoring, the overwrite cycle) exists to
    manage the risk of editing in place; a copy removes most of that risk by construction.

    THE ROWS COME FROM THE WORKSPACE, NOT FROM A DISK WALK. Measured on a real 19.1 workspace
    2026-08-05: declared folders 978 MB / 3,199 files, home directory 6,540 MB / 12,965 files. The
    5.5 GB difference is six dead "Data - *" / "DataNils" copies sitting beside the live one, and
    nothing on disk distinguishes them - only the configuration does. A recursive walk would copy
    all seven.

    READ THE .ws VERBATIM. The path keys live in the legacy .ws config that the .sws points at
    ([WorkspacePaths] ConfigFile=), and there are MORE of them than tWorkspacePaths models - that
    same workspace declares IdeSrcPath, which the struct has no field for. Driving the copy off the
    struct would silently drop a declared folder, so every key ending in "Path" becomes a row,
    including ones this build has never heard of. Silently-dropped is the worst failure this step
    can have.

    A STANDALONE CLASS extending cLibraryInventory - for the .sws/.ws section parsing and the
    [Libraries] chain, and because UnitTests builds a plain cApplication, so nothing on
    cRefactorApplication is reachable from the suite (see cLibraryInventory's own note).

    CHEAP, in the sense that matters: BuildCopyPlan never walks the COPY TREE. Sizes stay a separate
    per-row call (MeasureCopyItem) because a web tile's psHtml builder runs at first paint and a
    recursive walk there renders the tile blank - there is a test asserting bMeasured is False.

    It does now read the library CHAIN (LibraryCopyItems -> WorkspaceLibraryTree, 2026-08-14): a
    bounded set of small .sws files plus a directory search per library, the same walk the Libraries
    tile does. That is fine HERE because BuildCopyPlan has exactly one production caller -
    SelectCopyScopeFT's BuildPlan, once per Popup, cached in paCopyItems - and is not on a render
    path. If it ever becomes one, memoise it the way cFTLibraryReport.RefreshLibraryCounts does;
    recomputing that walk per paint is the shape of the pinned-indicator regression.
*/
Class cWorkspaceCopyPlan is a cLibraryInventory

    Procedure Construct_Object
        Forward Send Construct_Object
        // How many compiler-output files the last CopyFileList left out (see _IsCompilerOutput) - the
        // executor's report names the count, so a leaner copy never reads as a smaller one.
        Property Integer piCompilerOutputsSkipped 0
    End_Procedure
    // _IsAbsolutePath / RemoveFolderSeperator / _FolderOf / _ResolvePath / WorkspaceConfigFile /
    // WorkspaceHomeFromConfig are INHERITED from cLibraryInventory - they are .sws/.ws reading, so
    // they live with the rest of it and cWorkspaceFile gets them too.



    // Which eCopy* role a declared .ws key carries. Unknown "*Path" keys deliberately get their
    // own role rather than being folded into a neighbour - the point is that they stay VISIBLE.
    Function _RoleForConfigKey String sKey Returns Integer
        String sLow

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sKey))) to sLow
        If (sLow = "appsrcpath" or sLow = "ddsrcpath") ;
            Function_Return eCopySource
        // IdeSrcPath holds the Studio's own project files - NOT application source (Nils
        // 2026-08-05). It still travels with the workspace, but calling it "source" would put it in
        // the same class as AppSrc and invite it into decisions where it does not belong.
        If (sLow = "idesrcpath") ;
            Function_Return eCopyStudio
        If (sLow = "datapath") ;
            Function_Return eCopyData
        If (sLow = "programpath") ;
            Function_Return eCopyOutput
        If (sLow = "bitmappath" or sLow = "helppath" or sLow = "apphtmlpath") ;
            Function_Return eCopyResource
        Function_Return eCopyDeclaredOther
    End_Function

    /* A declared path that CANNOT be a copy row, however legitimately it is declared.

       Found in the wild 2026-08-05: a real .ws carries "Path=c:\". The rule that makes a row out of
       every key ending in "path" takes that literally, so the plan offered the WHOLE C: DRIVE - and
       Measure would then walk it and hang the dialog.

       Two shapes are refused:
         - a drive root ("C:\", "C:") - never a workspace folder, and unbounded to measure;
         - an ANCESTOR of the workspace home - copying it would copy the destination into itself.
       "Outside the home" is NOT one of them: an older workspace can legitimately declare
       AppSrcPath=C:\Program Files (x86)\..., and dropping that would silently lose real source. It
       is flagged instead (tCopyItem.bOutsideHome). */
    Function _IsUnusableDeclaredPath String sPath String sHome Returns Boolean
        String sLow sHomeLow

        Move (Trim(sPath)) to sLow
        If (sLow = "") ;
            Function_Return True
        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sLow))) to sLow
        // "c:" is what a drive root collapses to once the trailing separator is stripped.
        If (Length(sLow) <= 2) ;
            Function_Return True
        If (sHome = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sHome))) to sHomeLow
        // An ancestor of home: home starts with it, and it is shorter.
        If (Length(sLow) < Length(sHomeLow)) Begin
            If (Left(sHomeLow, Length(sLow) + 1) = (sLow + "\")) ;
                Function_Return True
        End
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    // Does sPath sit outside the workspace home? Real and common in older workspaces - reported,
    // never refused.
    Function _IsOutsideHome String sPath String sHome Returns Boolean
        String sLow sHomeLow
        If (sPath = "" or sHome = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sPath))) to sLow
        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sHome))) to sHomeLow
        If (sLow = sHomeLow) ;
            Function_Return False
        If (Left(sLow, Length(sHomeLow) + 1) = (sHomeLow + "\")) ;
            Function_Return False
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // A folder that must never be copied: DFRefactor's own backup trees (BOTH the underscore form
    // and the legacy form with a space) and version-control metadata. Copying a backup tree into a
    // migration target would double the size and carry stale originals into a new workspace.
    Function _IsExcludedFolderName String sName Returns Boolean
        String sLow
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sName))) to sLow
        If (sLow = Lowercase(CS_BackupFolder)) ;
            Function_Return True
        If (sLow = "dfrefactor backup") ;
            Function_Return True
        If (sLow = ".git" or sLow = ".svn" or sLow = ".hg") ;
            Function_Return True
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* A file that is the OUTPUT of a compile of the source workspace - a .prn listing, a .err message
       file, a .dep dependency list - and must not travel with the copy (Nils 2026-08-17). They are
       regenerated by any compile, so nothing is lost; and carried across they are actively wrong:
       the analyzer's "Show last Check" re-parses the plan's sSourcePrn, and the recopied WinServ
       came with the ORIGINAL's 55 MB WINSERV.prn plus three .err files from June - a compile that
       never happened in this copy, presented as its findings. Executables, .dbg and precompiled
       packages are left alone: they are outputs too, but they are what the copy RUNS with until it
       is rebuilt, and none of them feeds the analyzer. Extension match only, case-insensitive. */
    Function _IsCompilerOutput String sFile Returns Boolean
        String sExt
        Integer iPos
        Move (RightPos(".", sFile)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Lowercase(Mid(sFile, (Length(sFile) - iPos), (iPos + 1)))) to sExt
        Function_Return (sExt = "prn" or sExt = "err" or sExt = "dep")
    End_Function

    /* Measure ONE row of a plan and hand the whole plan back with that row filled in.

       ARRAY IN, ARRAY OUT - and that shape is forced, not stylistic. A method that returns a BARE
       struct cannot be called across objects: "Get <fn> of hoPlan ... to MyStruct" yields an empty
       struct, every field at its default, with no error raised. Proved 2026-08-05 - rfFolderSize
       reported the fixture's file correctly while the same value returned through a bare-struct
       method arrived as zeros. Struct ARRAYS do marshal (BuildCopyPlan returns one), so anything
       the view has to call lives behind an array-returning signature. A self-send inside the class
       is fine either way, which is exactly why _MakeCopyItem gets away with it.

       Also the right shape for the job: the view owns one plan array and walks it a row at a time,
       so the count and the running total can stream instead of freezing on a full-tree walk.

       RECURSIVE on disk - never call this from a web tile's psHtml builder, which runs at first
       paint; a heavy walk there renders the tile blank. */
    Function MeasureCopyItems tCopyItem[] aItems Integer iRow Returns tCopyItem[]
        tRFFolderSize lsSize
        String sPath
        Boolean bExists bIsLink

        If (iRow < 0 or iRow >= SizeOfArray(aItems)) ;
            Function_Return aItems

        Move aItems[iRow].sPath to sPath
        Move (vFolderExists(sPath) <> 0) to bExists
        Move (rfIsReparsePoint(sPath)) to bIsLink
        Move bExists to aItems[iRow].bExists
        Move bIsLink to aItems[iRow].bIsLink
        Move 0 to aItems[iRow].nBytes
        Move 0 to aItems[iRow].iFiles
        If (bExists = True and bIsLink = False) Begin
            // A link is reported, never measured - walking it would price a shared library pool as
            // if it lived inside this workspace.
            Move (rfFolderSize(sPath)) to lsSize
            Move lsSize.nBytes to aItems[iRow].nBytes
            Move lsSize.iFiles to aItems[iRow].iFiles
        End
        Move True to aItems[iRow].bMeasured
        Function_Return aItems
    End_Function

    // One row from a resolved folder. No disk I/O beyond an existence probe, so the plan stays
    // cheap; sizes arrive later via MeasureCopyItem.
    Function _MakeCopyItem String sKey String sPath Integer eRole Boolean bDeclared Boolean bSelected Returns tCopyItem
        tCopyItem Item
        String sLeaf
        Integer iPos

        Move sKey to Item.sKey
        Move (RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sPath)) to Item.sPath
        Move (RightPos("\", Item.sPath)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Mid(Item.sPath, (Length(Item.sPath)), (iPos + 1))) to sLeaf
        Else ;
            Move Item.sPath to sLeaf
        Move sLeaf to Item.sName
        Move eRole to Item.eRole
        Move bDeclared to Item.bDeclared
        Move (vFolderExists(Item.sPath) <> 0) to Item.bExists
        Move (rfIsReparsePoint(Item.sPath)) to Item.bIsLink
        Move bSelected to Item.bSelected
        Move False to Item.bMeasured
        Function_Return Item
    End_Function

    /* The rows the workspace itself declares: every "*Path" key in the .ws [Workspace] section.

       A path value may hold SEVERAL folders separated by ";" (the .sws permits multiples, which is
       why tWorkspacePaths documents its fields as "first entry where the .sws permits multiple").
       Each one becomes its own row - taking only the first would silently drop the rest. */
    Function DeclaredCopyItems String sSwsFile Returns tCopyItem[]
        tCopyItem[] aItems
        String[] asEntries asParts
        String sWsFile sHome sEntry sKey sValue sPart
        Integer i iMax j jMax iPos eRole iRow

        Get WorkspaceConfigFile sSwsFile to sWsFile
        If (sWsFile = "") ;
            Function_Return aItems
        Get WorkspaceHomeFromConfig sWsFile to sHome

        Get SwsSectionEntries sWsFile "Workspace" to asEntries
        Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move asEntries[i] to sEntry
            Move (Pos("=", sEntry)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 1) Begin
                Move (Trim(Left(sEntry, iPos - 1))) to sKey
                Move (Trim(Mid(sEntry, (Length(sEntry) - iPos), (iPos + 1)))) to sValue
                // "Home" anchors everything else and is not itself a row; "Description" and
                // "FileList" are not folders (FileList names a file INSIDE DataPath).
                If (Lowercase(sKey) <> "home" and Right(Lowercase(sKey), 4) = "path" and sValue <> "") Begin
                    Get _RoleForConfigKey sKey to eRole
                    Move (StrSplitToArray(sValue, ";")) to asParts
                    Move (SizeOfArray(asParts) - 1) to jMax
                    For j from 0 to jMax
                        Move (Trim(asParts[j])) to sPart
                        If (sPart <> "") Begin
                            Get _ResolvePath sHome sPart to sPart
                            // A drive root or an ancestor of home is refused outright - see
                            // _IsUnusableDeclaredPath for the "Path=c:\" case that forced this.
                            If (_IsUnusableDeclaredPath(Self, sPart, sHome) = False) Begin
                                Move (SizeOfArray(aItems)) to iRow
                                Get _MakeCopyItem sKey sPart eRole True True to aItems[iRow]
                                Move (_IsOutsideHome(Self, sPart, sHome)) to aItems[iRow].bOutsideHome
                            End
                        End
                    Loop
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aItems
    End_Function

    /* Library folders, from the .sws [Libraries] chain.

       Copied by DEFAULT, and that is deliberate. The entries are relative to home
       ("Lib1=Libraries\DFAbout\DFAbout19.1.sws"), so a copy that leaves them behind has three
       dangling references. Giving the copy its own library folders also makes the migration's
       central promise true: retargeting the copy cannot reach the original.

       A library folder that is a JUNCTION (the shared-pool model) is flagged, not followed - it
       still gets a row, so the view can offer the choice rather than duplicating a pool silently. */
    Function LibraryCopyItems String sSwsFile Returns tCopyItem[]
        tCopyItem[] aItems
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        String[] asSeenRoots
        String sRoot sNoKey sNoVersion sRootKey
        Integer i iMax iRow

        // sNoKey is a VARIABLE holding "", never the literal. An empty string literal passed as a
        // message argument is DROPPED by DataFlex: the remaining arguments shift left and the last
        // one is simply absent, which surfaces as error 57 "Required message argument missing"
        // inside the callee rather than at the call. Cost an Alt+P crash 2026-08-05.
        Move "" to sNoKey
        Move "" to sNoVersion
        /* THE WHOLE TREE, not just the workspace's own [Libraries] (Nils 2026-08-14).

           Libraries declare libraries. WinServ declares one - WebAppSrc - and WebAppSrc's own
           .sws declares seven more (vwin32fh, five otherdev ones, VDFQuery). Reading only the
           workspace's list meant those seven were never CANDIDATES for the copy: the flat reader
           cannot see them, so the dialog could not offer them and the copy could not take them.

           It usually looked fine because a sub-library normally lives INSIDE its parent's folder,
           where the parent's own recursive copy sweeps it up anyway - BuildCopyPlan drops such a
           row through _IsCovered, so nothing is copied twice. What that masked is the case this
           exists for: a sub-library sitting OUTSIDE its parent's root, which nothing would then
           copy and which df-cli later reports as a workspace that will not load.

           bFound only - a declaration pointing at nothing is a defect to report, not a folder to
           copy - and one row per ROOT, because several .sws files in one library resolve to the
           same folder. */
        Get WorkspaceLibraryTree sSwsFile sNoVersion CI_LibraryTreeMaxDepth to aLibs
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move "" to sRoot
            If (aLibs[i].bFound = True) ;
                Get LibraryRootFolder aLibs[i].sFolder to sRoot
            Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sRoot))) to sRootKey
            If (sRoot <> "" and (SearchArray(sRootKey, asSeenRoots)) <> -1) ;
                Move "" to sRoot
            If (sRoot <> "") Begin
                Move sRootKey to asSeenRoots[SizeOfArray(asSeenRoots)]
                // Straight into the array element, exactly as DeclaredCopyItems and
                // UndeclaredCopyItems do. Returning into a plain local struct and appending it
                // afterwards left every field at its default here - the row arrived with eRole 0
                // and bSelected False, and only LOOKED right because sName was overwritten below.
                Move (SizeOfArray(aItems)) to iRow
                Get _MakeCopyItem sNoKey sRoot eCopyLibrary True True to aItems[iRow]
                If (aLibs[i].sName <> "") ;
                    Move aLibs[i].sName to aItems[iRow].sName
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aItems
    End_Function

    // Is sPath already represented by (or inside) one of the rows in aItems?
    Function _IsCovered tCopyItem[] aItems String sPath Returns Boolean
        String sLow sRow
        Integer i iMax

        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sPath))) to sLow
        Move (SizeOfArray(aItems) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, aItems[i].sPath))) to sRow
            If (sRow = sLow) ;
                Function_Return True
            // A declared path nested under this folder means the folder itself is already spoken
            // for by a more precise row.
            If (Left(sRow, Length(sLow) + 1) = (sLow + "\")) ;
                Function_Return True
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* Everything else sitting directly under home - offered, but UNTICKED.

       This is the 5.5 GB category on a real workspace: six stale "Data - *" copies that nothing
       references. They are shown rather than hidden, because a user who really does need one must
       be able to say so; they are unticked, because nothing in the workspace claims them. */
    Function UndeclaredCopyItems String sSwsFile tCopyItem[] aKnown Returns tCopyItem[]
        tCopyItem[] aItems
        tRFSearchResult[] asDirs
        String sWsFile sHome sPath sName sNoKey
        Integer i iMax eRole
        Boolean bCovered

        // See LibraryCopyItems - an empty string LITERAL as a message argument gets dropped.
        Move "" to sNoKey

        Get WorkspaceConfigFile sSwsFile to sWsFile
        If (sWsFile = "") ;
            Function_Return aItems
        Get WorkspaceHomeFromConfig sWsFile to sHome
        If (sHome = "") ;
            Function_Return aItems

        Move (rfFileSearch((vFolderFormat(sHome) + "*.*"), DIRMODE_DIRECTORIES_ONLY, False)) to asDirs
        Move (SizeOfArray(asDirs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move asDirs[i].sFilename to sName
            Move (vFolderFormat(sHome) + sName) to sPath
            Get _IsCovered aKnown sPath to bCovered
            If (bCovered = False) Begin
                Move eCopyUndeclared to eRole
                If (_IsExcludedFolderName(Self, sName)) ;
                    Move eCopyExcluded to eRole
                Get _MakeCopyItem sNoKey sPath eRole False False to aItems[SizeOfArray(aItems)]
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aItems
    End_Function

    /* Add a hand-picked folder to a plan (the dialog's Ins / "Add Folder").

       ARRAY IN, ARRAY OUT for the marshalling reason documented on MeasureCopyItems - and the row
       is built straight into the array element, never into a local struct, which silently arrives
       empty.

       A folder already in the plan is TICKED rather than duplicated: the user asking for a folder
       that is already listed means "I want this copied", and answering with a second identical row
       would be a worse answer to the same request. */
    Function AddFolderToPlan tCopyItem[] aItems String sFolder Returns tCopyItem[]
        Integer i iMax iRow
        String sLow sRow sNoKey

        Move "" to sNoKey
        If (Trim(sFolder) = "") ;
            Function_Return aItems
        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, Trim(sFolder)))) to sLow
        Move (SizeOfArray(aItems) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, aItems[i].sPath))) to sRow
            If (sRow = sLow) Begin
                If (aItems[i].eRole <> eCopyExcluded) ;
                    Move True to aItems[i].bSelected
                Function_Return aItems
            End
        Loop

        Move (SizeOfArray(aItems)) to iRow
        Get _MakeCopyItem sNoKey sFolder eCopyAdded False True to aItems[iRow]
        Function_Return aItems
    End_Function

    // Drop one row from a plan, matched by PATH rather than index - the grid can be sorted by any
    // column, so a positional delete would remove whichever folder happened to sit there.
    Function RemoveFolderFromPlan tCopyItem[] aItems String sFolder Returns tCopyItem[]
        tCopyItem[] aKept
        Integer i iMax
        String sLow sRow

        If (Trim(sFolder) = "") ;
            Function_Return aItems
        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, Trim(sFolder)))) to sLow
        Move (SizeOfArray(aItems) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, aItems[i].sPath))) to sRow
            If (sRow <> sLow) ;
                Move aItems[i] to aKept[SizeOfArray(aKept)]
        Loop
        Function_Return aKept
    End_Function

    // sPath expressed as ".\rest" relative to sHome, or "" when it does not sit under sHome.
    Function _RelativeToHome String sPath String sHome Returns String
        String sLow sHomeLow sRest

        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sPath))) to sLow
        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sHome))) to sHomeLow
        If (sHomeLow = "" or sLow = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (sLow = sHomeLow) ;
            Function_Return ".\"
        If (Left(sLow, Length(sHomeLow) + 1) <> (sHomeLow + "\")) ;
            Function_Return ""
        // Take the tail from the ORIGINAL string so the caller keeps its own capitalisation.
        Move (Mid(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sPath), Length(sPath), Length(sHomeLow) + 2)) to sRest
        Function_Return (".\" + sRest)
    End_Function

    /* True when sPath sits BESIDE the home rather than inside it - a sibling library folder
       (..\vdfquery) or something under a sibling pool (..\Libraries\DataFlex Reports 5.0.1).

       Such a row is copied to the same position beside the NEW home, which has two consequences
       the callers depend on: the .ws / .sws entry naming it needs NO rewriting (the very string
       "..\Libraries\X" resolves against the copy just as it did against the original), and the
       destination is a folder other workspaces may already share - so the copy CREATES it or
       leaves it alone, and never merges into it.

       Excludes the parent itself: mirroring ".\" would copy everything beside the workspace.

       AND EXCLUDES HOME'S OWN SUBTREE, which is the whole point of the name and was missing until
       2026-08-06. "Under the parent" is true of everything under HOME as well - home is itself
       under its parent - so `E:\LegacyApp10\AppSrc` tested against home `E:\GP` came back True: a sibling
       library. DestinationForRow never noticed, because it resolves inside-home FIRST and returns;
       the damage landed on _UsesExistingSibling, which asks this question on its own.

       The consequence was not a wording slip. On a SECOND copy to a destination that already held
       the first one, every one of the workspace's own folders - AppSrc, Data, DDSrc, Programs -
       matched "an existing folder beside the destination", so CopyFileList dropped them and the
       re-copy silently refreshed nothing (Nils 2026-08-06: "it is only Libraries\vdfquery that is
       a library, the rest should be copied again"). A skip meant for folders the workspace does
       not own was being applied to the ones it does.

       Inside home the answer is always False and the caller's own inside-home handling takes over:
       those folders belong to this workspace, so they are copied - and if something is already at
       the destination the newer-file guard decides per FILE, which is the right granularity for
       content we own. */
    Function _MirrorsBesideHome String sPath String sHome Returns Boolean
        String sParent sRel

        // Inside home - including home itself, which _RelativeToHome answers ".\" - is not beside it.
        Get _RelativeToHome sPath sHome to sRel
        If (sRel <> "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _FolderOf (RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sHome)) to sParent
        If (sParent = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Get _RelativeToHome sPath sParent to sRel
        Function_Return (sRel <> "" and sRel <> ".\")
    End_Function

    /* The rewritten [Workspace] section for a COPIED workspace.

       WHY THIS EXISTS AND IS NOT OPTIONAL. A .ws may declare its folders as ABSOLUTE paths - found
       on this machine 2026-08-05, and not rarely: one real file declares every single path that way
       (AppSrcPath=C:\Visual DataFlex Projects\OldApp\AppSrc). Copy such a workspace anywhere and
       the copy's .ws still points at the ORIGINAL folders. It would compile and run against the
       original's source and data, and refactoring the "copy" would edit the originals - exactly the
       accident that copying first is meant to make impossible.

       The rule, per line:
         - a path that resolves INSIDE the old home  -> rewritten as ".\rest", so it follows the copy
           (this covers both the already-relative case and the absolute-inside-home case);
         - a path that resolves OUTSIDE the old home -> depends on the PLAN. A ticked row is copied
           under the new home by its leaf name (DestinationForRow), so the entry is repointed at
           ".\leaf" and the copy becomes self-contained. An unticked one is left EXACTLY as written:
           it was not copied, so repointing would aim at nothing, and the copy legitimately shares it
           with the original - PathsSharedWithOriginal names those so the user is told;
         - Home=, Description= and anything unrecognised -> passed through untouched.

       Returns the whole file's lines, so the caller writes it out verbatim. */
    Function RewrittenWorkspaceLines String sSwsFile String sNewHome tCopyItem[] aItems Returns String[]
        String[] asLines asOut
        String sWsFile sHome sLine sTrim sKey sValue sAbs sRel sLeaf
        Integer i iMax iPos

        Get WorkspaceConfigFile sSwsFile to sWsFile
        If (sWsFile = "") ;
            Function_Return asOut
        Get WorkspaceHomeFromConfig sWsFile to sHome
        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sWsFile)) to asLines

        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move asLines[i] to sLine
            Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(13), sLine, ""))) to sTrim
            Move (Pos("=", sTrim)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 1 and Left(sTrim, 1) <> "[" and Left(sTrim, 1) <> ";") Begin
                Move (Trim(Left(sTrim, iPos - 1))) to sKey
                Move (Trim(Mid(sTrim, (Length(sTrim) - iPos), (iPos + 1)))) to sValue
                If (Lowercase(sKey) <> "home" and sValue <> "" and ;
                    (Right(Lowercase(sKey), 4) = "path" or Lowercase(sKey) = "filelist")) Begin
                    Get _ResolvePath sHome sValue to sAbs
                    Get _RelativeToHome sAbs sHome to sRel
                    If (sRel <> "") Begin
                        Move (sKey + "=" + sRel) to sLine
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        // Outside the home: repoint only if the plan says it travels, AND it
                        // travels to a different relative position. A row that lands BESIDE the
                        // new home keeps the exact path it was written with - "..\Libraries\X"
                        // resolves against the copy just as it did against the original, so
                        // rewriting it would break what already works.
                        Get _SelectedLeafFor aItems sAbs to sLeaf
                        If (sLeaf <> "" and _MirrorsBesideHome(Self, sAbs, sHome) = False) ;
                            Move (sKey + "=.\" + sLeaf) to sLine
                    End
                End
            End
            Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        Loop
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    // The leaf name an out-of-home path will be copied to, or "" when the plan does not carry it.
    Function _SelectedLeafFor tCopyItem[] aItems String sPath Returns String
        String sLow sRow sLeaf
        Integer i iMax iPos

        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sPath))) to sLow
        Move (SizeOfArray(aItems) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, aItems[i].sPath))) to sRow
            If (sRow = sLow and aItems[i].bSelected = True) Begin
                Move (RightPos("\", sRow)) to iPos
                If (iPos = 0) ;
                    Function_Return ""
                Move (RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, aItems[i].sPath)) to sLeaf
                Function_Return (Right(sLeaf, (Length(sLeaf) - iPos)))
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* The declared paths a copy would still SHARE with the original - absolute, outside the home,
       and therefore not rewritable. Each entry reads "<Key>=<value>".

       Surfaced rather than silently accepted: sharing a Data folder between the original and its
       migration copy is sometimes exactly right and sometimes a disaster, and only the user knows
       which. Saying nothing would let the copy look independent when it is not. */
    Function PathsSharedWithOriginal String sSwsFile tCopyItem[] aItems Returns String[]
        String[] asLines asOut
        String sWsFile sHome sTrim sKey sValue sAbs sRel sLeaf
        Integer i iMax iPos

        Get WorkspaceConfigFile sSwsFile to sWsFile
        If (sWsFile = "") ;
            Function_Return asOut
        Get WorkspaceHomeFromConfig sWsFile to sHome
        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sWsFile)) to asLines

        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(13), asLines[i], ""))) to sTrim
            Move (Pos("=", sTrim)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 1 and Left(sTrim, 1) <> "[" and Left(sTrim, 1) <> ";") Begin
                Move (Trim(Left(sTrim, iPos - 1))) to sKey
                Move (Trim(Mid(sTrim, (Length(sTrim) - iPos), (iPos + 1)))) to sValue
                If (Lowercase(sKey) <> "home" and sValue <> "" and ;
                    (Right(Lowercase(sKey), 4) = "path" or Lowercase(sKey) = "filelist")) Begin
                    Get _ResolvePath sHome sValue to sAbs
                    Get _RelativeToHome sAbs sHome to sRel
                    If (sRel = "") Begin
                        // A TICKED out-of-home path travels with the copy and was repointed by
                        // RewrittenWorkspaceLines, so it is not shared - reporting it anyway would
                        // contradict what the copy actually did.
                        Get _SelectedLeafFor aItems sAbs to sLeaf
                        If (sLeaf = "") ;
                            Move (sKey + "=" + sValue) to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    // "19.1" -> "191". Some library headers spell the version without the dot (vWin32fh-Library-DF191).
    Function _VersionCompact String sVer Returns String
        Function_Return (Replaces(".", Trim(sVer), ""))
    End_Function

    /* The FILENAME for a library .sws header that does not exist yet.

       Filename conventions across libraries are irreconcilable - three libraries, three schemes, and
       vwin32fh changed its own - so nothing here PARSES a filename to learn a version. It works the
       other way round: the template's version is known from INSIDE the file, and that known string is
       substituted where it appears in the name.

         AlphaLibrary19.1.sws      + 19.1 -> 26.0  =>  AlphaLibrary26.0.sws
         vWin32fh-Library-DF191.sws + 19.1 -> 26.0  =>  vWin32fh-Library-DF260.sws   (compact form)
         something-with-no-version  + ...          =>  <stem> DF26.0.sws             (appended)

       The appended fallback is deliberately plain: when the scheme cannot be recognised, inventing a
       clever name is worse than an obvious one. */
    Function SynthesizedLibrarySwsName String sTemplateFile String sTemplateVersion String sTargetVersion Returns String
        String sName sStem sExt sFrom sTo
        Integer iPos

        If (sTemplateFile = "" or sTargetVersion = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (RightPos("\", sTemplateFile)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Right(sTemplateFile, (Length(sTemplateFile) - iPos))) to sName
        Else ;
            Move sTemplateFile to sName

        Move (RightPos(".", sName)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 1) Begin
            Move (Left(sName, iPos - 1)) to sStem
            Move (Right(sName, (Length(sName) - iPos + 1))) to sExt
        End
        Else Begin
            Move sName to sStem
            Move ".sws" to sExt
        End

        If (Trim(sTemplateVersion) <> "") Begin
            // Dotted form first - it is the more specific of the two.
            Move (Trim(sTemplateVersion)) to sFrom
            Move (Trim(sTargetVersion)) to sTo
            If (sStem contains sFrom) ;
                Function_Return (Replaces(sFrom, sStem, sTo) + sExt)
            Get _VersionCompact sFrom to sFrom
            Get _VersionCompact sTo to sTo
            If (sFrom <> "" and sStem contains sFrom) ;
                Function_Return (Replaces(sFrom, sStem, sTo) + sExt)
        End
        Function_Return (sStem + " DF" + Trim(sTargetVersion) + sExt)
    End_Function

    /* Remove a product-and-version token from a name stem: "DF" or "VDF" together with a number.

       "_DF25.0", " DF191", "-DF260", and - Nils 2026-08-14 - " VDF 18.0", where "VDF" is the old
       Visual DataFlex name and the number is separated from it by a space. The whole thing is the
       version marker, so the whole thing goes: "Acme-UMS VDF 18.0" is the workspace Acme-UMS, and
       nothing else.

       It is also the suffix MigratedSwsFileName itself appends, so a workspace copied twice would
       otherwise accumulate them ("MyApp_DF25.0_DF26.0").

       Deliberately NOT a general digit-stripper: this file's whole naming philosophy is that a
       filename is never PARSED to learn a version (see SynthesizedLibrarySwsName), and "Route180"
       must survive intact - there is a test that says so.

       Three guards keep it honest:
         - a DIGIT must follow, after optional separators, so "ExportPDF" and a bare "MyApp DF" are
           untouched - "together with a number" is the rule, and no number means no token;
         - the token must start at the beginning, after a separator, OR run to the END of the stem.
           That last case is what makes "WinServDF19" - a real workspace, hit on 2026-08-14 - become
           "WinServ", while "MyDF25Tool" keeps its letters: a version marker is a SUFFIX or a word of
           its own, never something with a name carrying on after it;
         - "VDF" is tried BEFORE "DF", or the V would be left stranded.

       Only the FIRST such token is removed; a second would mean a name this function never wrote. */
    { Visibility=Private }
    Function _StripDfVersionToken String sStem Returns String
        String  sCh sPrev sHead sTail
        Integer iLen i iAt iTokLen iEnd iDigits
        Boolean bFound bStop bAtBoundary

        Move (Length(sStem)) to iLen
        If (iLen < 3) ;
            Function_Return sStem

        Move 0 to iAt
        Move 0 to iEnd
        Move False to bFound
        Move 1 to i
        While (i <= iLen and bFound = False)
            Move "" to sPrev
            If (i > 1) ;
                Move (Mid(sStem, 1, i - 1)) to sPrev        // the character right before the token
            Move (i = 1 or sPrev = "_" or sPrev = "-" or sPrev = " ") to bAtBoundary
            /* "VDF" first - testing "DF" first would match its last two letters and strand the V -
               but ONLY at a word boundary. Without that gate "WinServDF19" matches "vDF" at the v
               (uppercased, it IS "VDF") and comes out as "WinSer": the product name is a word of its
               own, while a v sitting in front of DF inside a word is just a letter. Cost a failing
               test to find, 2026-08-14. */
            Move 0 to iTokLen
            If (bAtBoundary = True and Uppercase(Mid(sStem, 3, i)) = "VDF") ;
                Move 3 to iTokLen
            Else If (Uppercase(Mid(sStem, 2, i)) = "DF") ;
                Move 2 to iTokLen
            If (iTokLen > 0) Begin
                // Separators between the token and its number ("VDF 18.0").
                Move (i + iTokLen) to iEnd
                Move False to bStop
                While (iEnd <= iLen and bStop = False)
                    Move (Mid(sStem, 1, iEnd)) to sCh
                    If (sCh = " " or sCh = "_" or sCh = "-") ;
                        Increment iEnd
                    Else ;
                        Move True to bStop
                Loop
                // The number itself. A dot counts only once digits have started, so a token
                // followed by ".something" cannot swallow the rest of the name.
                Move 0 to iDigits
                Move False to bStop
                While (iEnd <= iLen and bStop = False)
                    Move (Mid(sStem, 1, iEnd)) to sCh
                    If (sCh >= "0" and sCh <= "9") Begin
                        Increment iDigits
                        Increment iEnd
                    End
                    Else If (sCh = "." and iDigits > 0) Begin
                        Increment iEnd
                    End
                    Else ;
                        Move True to bStop
                Loop
                /* ACCEPT at a word boundary, or when the token runs to the END of the stem.
                   The second case is "WinServDF19" - no separator before the DF, but nothing after
                   the number either, so it is a suffix and not part of a word. "MyDF25Tool" fails
                   both tests and keeps its letters, which is the distinction that matters. */
                If (iDigits > 0 and (bAtBoundary = True or iEnd > iLen)) Begin
                    Move i to iAt
                    Move True to bFound
                End
            End
            Increment i
        Loop
        If (bFound = False) ;
            Function_Return sStem

        Move "" to sHead
        If (iAt > 1) ;
            Move (Left(sStem, iAt - 1)) to sHead
        Move "" to sTail
        If (iEnd <= iLen) ;
            Move (Mid(sStem, (iLen - iEnd + 1), iEnd)) to sTail
        Function_Return (sHead + sTail)
    End_Function

    /* The filename for the COPY's .sws.

       "Acme-UMS VDF 18.0.sws" migrating to 25.0 became "Acme-UMS VDF 18.0 DF25.0.sws" - stating two
       versions, one of them wrong, and carrying spaces (Nils 2026-08-05). Four rules:

         1. a "DF"/"VDF" token carrying a number is removed - both the suffix this function wrote on
            an earlier copy and the old "VDF 18.0" product-and-version spelling. First, because rule
            2 would otherwise take the number and strand the letters;
         2. the SOURCE version is removed. Which token that is comes from INSIDE the file, never from
            reading the name - the same rule the library headers follow. The compact form ("180") is
            tried only when the dotted one is absent, so a name that merely contains those digits is
            not mangled;
         3. spaces become underscores, and runs of them collapse - a workspace path with spaces is
            legal but a nuisance in every command line it later appears in;
         4. the target version is appended once, as "_DF<ver>" - but ONLY below
            CI_DfUnversionedSwsFrom.

       RULE 4 STOPS AT DATAFLEX 26 (Nils 2026-08-14). From DF26 the workspace carries its own version
       inside the JSON .sws and libraries/packages are resolved by the package handler, so a suffix
       would restate what the file already says and could later contradict it - the suffix is frozen
       at copy time, the "df" value is what the tooling reads. See CI_DfUnversionedSwsFrom.

       "Acme-UMS VDF 18.0.sws" + 18.0 -> 25.0  =>  "Acme-UMS_DF25.0.sws"
       "Acme-UMS VDF 18.0.sws" + 18.0 -> 26.0  =>  "Acme-UMS.sws"
       "MyApp_DF25.0.sws"     + 25.0 -> 26.0  =>  "MyApp.sws" */
    Function MigratedSwsFileName String sSourceSwsFile String sSourceVersion String sTargetVersion Returns String
        String sStem sFrom sOriginal
        Integer iPos iGuard iTargetKey

        If (sSourceSwsFile = "" or sTargetVersion = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (RightPos("\", sSourceSwsFile)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Right(sSourceSwsFile, (Length(sSourceSwsFile) - iPos))) to sStem
        Else ;
            Move sSourceSwsFile to sStem
        Move (RightPos(".", sStem)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 1) ;
            Move (Left(sStem, iPos - 1)) to sStem
        // Kept so the removals below can never strip a name down to nothing: a workspace called
        // "DF26.0.sws" is all version and no name, and ".sws" is not a filename.
        Move sStem to sOriginal

        /* 1. drop a "DF<ver>" token an earlier copy left behind, so the suffixes cannot stack.

              BEFORE the source-version removal, not after: for "MyApp_DF25.0" with 25.0 inside the
              file, removing the version first leaves "MyApp_DF" - a stranded "DF" this pass can no
              longer recognise, because the digits that identified it are gone. */
        Get _StripDfVersionToken sStem to sStem

        // 2. drop the source version token
        Move (Trim(sSourceVersion)) to sFrom
        If (sFrom <> "") Begin
            If (sStem contains sFrom) Begin
                Move (Replaces(sFrom, sStem, "")) to sStem
            End
            Else Begin
                Get _VersionCompact sFrom to sFrom
                If (sFrom <> "" and sStem contains sFrom) ;
                    Move (Replaces(sFrom, sStem, "")) to sStem
            End
        End

        // 3. spaces -> underscores, then collapse the runs the removal above tends to leave
        Move (Replaces(" ", sStem, "_")) to sStem
        Move 0 to iGuard
        While (sStem contains "__" and iGuard < 40)
            Move (Replaces("__", sStem, "_")) to sStem
            Increment iGuard
        Loop
        // Trim the separators a removed token can strand at either end.
        While (Right(sStem, 1) = "_" or Right(sStem, 1) = "-")
            Move (Left(sStem, Length(sStem) - 1)) to sStem
        Loop
        While (Left(sStem, 1) = "_")
            Move (Mid(sStem, Length(sStem), 2)) to sStem
        Loop

        If (Trim(sStem) = "") ;
            Move sOriginal to sStem

        // 4. the target version, unless the target is DF26 or later - where the file says it itself.
        Get _VersionKey sTargetVersion to iTargetKey
        If (iTargetKey >= CI_DfUnversionedSwsFrom) ;
            Function_Return (sStem + ".sws")
        Function_Return (sStem + "_DF" + Trim(sTargetVersion) + ".sws")
    End_Function

    /* The library headers that must be WRITTEN because the library ships none for the target.

       The template is the .sws the workspace actually references - the build it is known to work
       with - copied with its [Properties] Version= changed. That is the same edit the library author
       makes when supporting a new release.

       Paths are returned relative to the library folders as they stand NOW; the caller writing into
       a COPY must redirect them, because adding a header to the ORIGINAL library folder would modify
       the workspace this whole exercise exists to leave alone. */
    Function LibrarySwsToAdd String sSwsFile String sTargetVersion Returns tLibrarySwsToAdd[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        tLibrarySwsToAdd[] aOut
        String sNewName
        Integer i iMax iRow iTargetKey

        If (sTargetVersion = "") ;
            Function_Return aOut
        /* NOTHING TO WRITE FROM DF26 UP, because nothing will reference it. The header exists only
           to be named by a retargeted [Libraries] entry, and _RetargetedLibraryEntry stops
           renaming at 26 - so synthesising one here would put an unreferenced file into the copy
           and call it progress. Bringing a library forward to DF26 is the Libraries dialog's job,
           where it is a decision rather than a guess. */
        Get _VersionKey sTargetVersion to iTargetKey
        If (iTargetKey >= CI_DfUnversionedSwsFrom) ;
            Function_Return aOut
        Get WorkspaceLibraryInventory sSwsFile sTargetVersion to aLibs
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibNoLocalBuild) Begin
                Get SynthesizedLibrarySwsName aLibs[i].sRefSwsFile aLibs[i].sRefVersion sTargetVersion to sNewName
                If (sNewName <> "") Begin
                    Move (SizeOfArray(aOut)) to iRow
                    Move aLibs[i].sName        to aOut[iRow].sLibName
                    Move aLibs[i].sRefSwsFile  to aOut[iRow].sTemplateFile
                    Move (aLibs[i].sFolder + sNewName) to aOut[iRow].sNewFile
                    Move sTargetVersion        to aOut[iRow].sVersion
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aOut
    End_Function

    /* The [Libraries] value retargeted to sTargetVersion, or "" when it cannot be.

       The entry is home-relative ("Libraries\DFAbout\DFAbout19.1.sws"), and the target build lives
       in the SAME folder as the one referenced - so retargeting keeps the entry's own folder part
       and swaps only the filename. That matters: rebuilding the path from the resolved absolute one
       would turn a relative entry into an absolute one, and an absolute [Libraries] entry does not
       survive being copied. */
    Function _RetargetedLibraryEntry String sRawEntry String sHome tLibraryInfo[] aLibs String sTargetVersion Returns String
        String sResolved sRefLow sNewFile sFolderPart
        Integer i iMax iPos iTargetKey

        /* FROM DATAFLEX 26, A LIBRARY FILENAME IS NOT OURS TO REWRITE (Nils 2026-08-14).

           "The .sws file names for libraries should never be touched when the Target version is
           DF 26 and up. That seems to me is the bug in the logic here." It is.

           Below 26 the version lives in the filename, so retargeting a copy means renaming the
           entry and writing the header it now names - the pair below. From 26 the workspace
           declares DEPENDENCIES and the package handler resolves them; a versioned filename is
           whatever the library author happened to ship, not a name this program can derive.

           Deriving it anyway is what broke E:\WinServ. The synthesised WebAppSrc header had its
           nested entry rewritten from vWin32fh-Library-DF191.sws to -DF260, a file that exists
           nowhere - vwin32fh ships DF181/182/190/191/200 and no DF260 - so df-cli refused to load
           the workspace at all. An entry left as written at least RESOLVES; a workspace that will
           not load cannot even be converted, let alone have a package added.

           Returning "" means "no change": NewSwsLines keeps the line exactly as it read it. */
        Get _VersionKey sTargetVersion to iTargetKey
        If (iTargetKey >= CI_DfUnversionedSwsFrom) ;
            Function_Return ""

        Get ResolveLibraryPath sRawEntry sHome to sResolved
        Move (Lowercase(sResolved)) to sRefLow
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Lowercase(aLibs[i].sRefSwsFile) = sRefLow) Begin
                If (aLibs[i].sTargetSwsFile <> "") Begin
                    If (Lowercase(aLibs[i].sTargetSwsFile) = sRefLow) ;
                        Function_Return ""      // already the target build - nothing to change
                    Move (RightPos("\", aLibs[i].sTargetSwsFile)) to iPos
                    Move (Right(aLibs[i].sTargetSwsFile, (Length(aLibs[i].sTargetSwsFile) - iPos))) to sNewFile
                End
                Else Begin
                    // No build for the target ships with this library, so a header is SYNTHESISED
                    // (LibrarySwsToAdd writes it). The entry has to name the file that will exist,
                    // not the one that does - otherwise the new workspace points at the old build
                    // and the synthesised header is dead weight.
                    Get SynthesizedLibrarySwsName aLibs[i].sRefSwsFile aLibs[i].sRefVersion sTargetVersion to sNewFile
                    If (sNewFile = "") ;
                        Function_Return ""
                End
                Move (RightPos("\", sRawEntry)) to iPos
                If (iPos > 0) ;
                    Move (Left(sRawEntry, iPos)) to sFolderPart
                Else ;
                    Move "" to sFolderPart
                Function_Return (sFolderPart + sNewFile)
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* The .sws for the copy: [Properties] Version= set to the target, and every [Libraries] entry
       repointed at that library's build for the target where one exists locally.

       BOTH changes belong here. The .sws is the file that states which DataFlex version a workspace
       is for, and its [Libraries] block is where the library versions are named - so a copy created
       "for DF 26" whose libraries still point at the 19.1 builds is not a DF 26 workspace, it just
       claims to be. Nils 2026-08-05.

       A library with NO local build for the target is left exactly as it was, not guessed at and not
       dropped - the workspace still opens, and LibrariesWithoutTargetBuild names them so the user
       knows which ones need fetching. That is the eLibNoLocalBuild verdict doing its job: the long
       hop always ends at "get a newer library", and this refuses to pretend otherwise.

       A missing [Properties]/Version= is ADDED - a .sws without one cannot state its target. */
    /* NewSwsLines with a DROP list: the [Libraries] entries named in asDropRaw (as written in the
       source .sws) are left out of the copy's file, and the survivors are renumbered from Lib1 -
       the Studio reads Lib1, Lib2... in sequence, so a gap ends the list early. The drop list is
       what UncopiedInsideHomeLibraries found: a library inside the original's home that the copy
       leaves behind (Nils 2026-09-03). An empty list is plain NewSwsLines. */
    Function NewSwsLinesDropping String sSwsFile String sTargetVersion String[] asDropRaw Returns String[]
        String[] asLines asOut asDropLow
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        String sLine sTrim sKey sValue sHome sNewValue
        Integer i iMax iPos iFormat iLibNo
        Boolean bInProps bInLibs bDone bSkipLine

        If (sTargetVersion = "") ;
            Function_Return asOut
        Move (SizeOfArray(asDropRaw) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(asDropRaw[i]))) to asDropLow[i]
        Loop
        Move 0 to iLibNo

        /* REFUSE a JSON .sws rather than damage it.

           Everything below is INI surgery: it finds [Properties] to rewrite Version=, finds
           [Libraries] to repoint the entries, and passes every other line through. On a JSON
           document none of those headers exist, so nothing is rewritten AND - worse - the
           "no Version= found" fallback at the foot appends [Properties]/Version= to the end of a
           JSON file, producing something neither the Studio nor this tool can read. A silently
           wrong answer is bad; a corrupted workspace file is worse, and this is the only path here
           that can destroy rather than merely mislead.

           The test is on the FILE, not the caller, which is what makes it correct for both users of
           this function: a workspace .sws may be JSON (the DF26 Studio converts it), while the
           library headers NewLibrarySwsLines writes are always INI.

           Empty return = "not written". Callers already treat an empty array that way, and
           RunWorkspaceCopy says so plainly rather than reporting a copy it did not make. */
        Get SwsFormatOf sSwsFile to iFormat
        If (iFormat = eSwsJson) ;
            Function_Return asOut

        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sSwsFile)) to asLines
        Get WorkspaceLibraryInventory sSwsFile sTargetVersion to aLibs
        Get _FolderOf sSwsFile to sHome
        Move False to bInProps
        Move False to bInLibs
        Move False to bDone

        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move asLines[i] to sLine
            Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(13), sLine, ""))) to sTrim
            Move False to bSkipLine
            If (Left(sTrim, 1) = "[") Begin
                Move (Lowercase(sTrim) = "[properties]") to bInProps
                Move (Lowercase(sTrim) = "[libraries]") to bInLibs
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (Pos("=", sTrim)) to iPos
                If (iPos > 1) Begin
                    Move (Trim(Left(sTrim, iPos - 1))) to sKey
                    Move (Trim(Mid(sTrim, (Length(sTrim) - iPos), (iPos + 1)))) to sValue
                    If (bInProps = True and bDone = False and Lowercase(sKey) = "version") Begin
                        Move ("Version=" + sTargetVersion) to sLine
                        Move True to bDone
                    End
                    If (bInLibs = True and sValue <> "") Begin
                        // DROPPED, not repointed: the copy has no folder for it (see the drop list).
                        If (SearchArray(Lowercase(Trim(sValue)), asDropLow) <> -1) ;
                            Move True to bSkipLine
                        Else Begin
                            Get _RetargetedLibraryEntry sValue sHome aLibs sTargetVersion to sNewValue
                            If (sNewValue = "") ;
                                Move sValue to sNewValue
                            Increment iLibNo
                            // Only a copy that DROPS something renumbers; otherwise every line
                            // goes through exactly as it was read, keys included.
                            If (SizeOfArray(asDropLow) > 0) ;
                                Move ("Lib" + String(iLibNo) + "=" + sNewValue) to sLine
                            Else If (sNewValue <> sValue) ;
                                Move (sKey + "=" + sNewValue) to sLine
                        End
                    End
                End
            End
            If (bSkipLine = False) ;
                Move sLine to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        Loop

        If (bDone = False) Begin
            Move "[Properties]" to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            Move ("Version=" + sTargetVersion) to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        End
        /* EVERY PROGRAM GOES INTO THE COPY (Nils 2026-09-11). A [Projects] section whose slots
           repeat a number, or skip one, is renumbered Project0..N here with every program kept -
           the copy's .sws is the file a DataFlex 26 conversion reads next, and df-cli keeps only
           the last of two lines that share a key. A healthy section comes through untouched. See
           SwsIniProjectsFromLines in cLibraryInventory.pkg. */
        Move (SwsIniLinesWithProjectsRenumbered(asOut)) to asOut
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    // The plain form - nothing dropped. Defined AFTER the worker: an expression call needs it declared first.
    Function NewSwsLines String sSwsFile String sTargetVersion Returns String[]
        String[] asNone
        Function_Return (NewSwsLinesDropping(Self, sSwsFile, sTargetVersion, asNone))
    End_Function

    /* The CONTENT of a synthesised library header: the template's lines with [Properties] Version=
       changed to the target.

       The same rule the workspace's own .sws follows, and for the same reason - a header that names
       one version while sitting in a file named for another is the exact confusion the "never parse
       the filename, read the version inside" rule exists to prevent. NewSwsLines does the work;
       this is the library-facing name for it. */
    Function NewLibrarySwsLines String sTemplateSwsFile String sTargetVersion Returns String[]
        String[] asOut
        Get NewSwsLines sTemplateSwsFile sTargetVersion to asOut
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    // Libraries with no build for the target anywhere in their own folder - the ones the user has to
    // fetch. Named, never guessed at: see the eLibNoLocalBuild note in cLibraryInventory.
    /* Library references anywhere in the copy's chain that do not resolve to a file on disk - or
       resolve to one that cannot be read as a workspace file (git conflict markers, 2026-09-14).

       WHY THE WHOLE TREE AND NOT JUST [Libraries] (Nils 2026-08-14, from a real hang). The copy of
       C:\Projects\DF19\WinServ declared one library, and it resolved. What did not resolve was one
       level DOWN: the synthesised WebAppSrc-Library-DF260.sws names seven nested libraries of its
       own - vwin32fh, five otherdev ones, VDFQuery - and none of those DF260 files exist. A
       one-level check would have declared that copy healthy.

       The chain was ALREADY broken at the source: vWin32fh-Library-DF191.sws is missing beside the
       original too. So this is not the copy failing, it is the copy faithfully carrying a defect
       forward - which is exactly why the answer is to TELL the user rather than to try to repair
       anything.

       Same depth as the Libraries report, so the two cannot disagree about what "declared" means.
       Reports the entry AS WRITTEN, because that is the string the user will search the .sws for. */
    Function UnresolvableLibraryRefs String sSwsFile String sTargetVersion Returns String[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        String[] asBad
        Integer i iMax
        String sWhich

        Get WorkspaceLibraryTree sSwsFile sTargetVersion CI_LibraryTreeMaxDepth to aLibs
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs)) to iMax
        Decrement iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            /* PACKAGES ARE NOT MISSING FILES. For an eDepPackage entry bFound means "is that exact
               version installed in this cache" (LibraryEntriesInfo), not "does a file exist" - a
               package that is simply not installed here is not a broken reference, and listing it
               as one would blame the workspace for the state of a machine. */
            If ((aLibs[i].bFound = False or aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibUnreadable) and aLibs[i].eKind <> eDepPackage) Begin
                Move (Trim(aLibs[i].sRawEntry)) to sWhich
                If (sWhich = "") ;
                    Move (Trim(aLibs[i].sRefSwsFile)) to sWhich
                // A file that IS there but carries git conflict markers says so, or the reader goes
                // looking for a folder that exists (2026-09-14, see eLibUnreadable).
                If (sWhich <> "" and aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibUnreadable) ;
                    Move (sWhich + "  - the file is there but carries git conflict markers (an unfinished merge); resolve it in that repository") to sWhich
                If (sWhich <> "") ;
                    Move sWhich to asBad[SizeOfArray(asBad)]
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return asBad
    End_Function

    Function LibrariesWithoutTargetBuild String sSwsFile String sTargetVersion Returns String[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        String[] asOut
        Integer i iMax

        If (sTargetVersion = "") ;
            Function_Return asOut
        Get WorkspaceLibraryInventory sSwsFile sTargetVersion to aLibs
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibNoLocalBuild) ;
                Move aLibs[i].sName to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
        Loop
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    /* Where one plan row lands under the new home. Three cases, by where the row sits relative
       to the ORIGINAL home - the copy keeps the shape the workspace was built with.

       INSIDE the old home -> the same relative position. An internal library stays internal.

       BESIDE it - a sibling library (..\vdfquery) or one inside a sibling pool
       (..\Libraries\DataFlex Reports 5.0.1) -> the same position beside the NEW home, so a
       sibling stays a sibling and a pooled library stays pooled. This is what keeps the .ws and
       .sws entries valid WITHOUT rewriting them, and it is why the executor may skip such a
       folder when the destination already has it: a pool beside the destination can belong to
       other workspaces, and copying into it would overwrite their files with this workspace's
       older ones.

       ANYWHERE ELSE (C:\Program Files\..., another drive) -> directly under the new home by its
       leaf name, which is what makes the copy SELF-CONTAINED. That is the whole point of copying:
       a workspace whose AppSrc lives in C:\Program Files would otherwise leave the "copy"
       refactoring the original's source. */
    /* Where a FILE that sat at sOldFile ends up in the copy - the same three-branch rule
       DestinationForRow applies to folders, because it has to be: a file lands wherever its folder
       landed, and any second implementation of that mapping is a bug waiting for the case the two
       disagree on.

       IT ALREADY DISAGREED. The synthesised library headers were placed with RelativeInsideHome,
       which answers only the ENCLOSED case and returns "" for everything else - so for a SIBLING
       library ("..\Example Library\") the header was silently skipped while NewSwsLines had already
       retargeted the workspace's [Libraries] entry to name it. The result was a .sws pointing at a
       file nobody wrote: df-cli reports "State of <workspace>: BROKEN", and it is broken (found on
       a real 19.1 -> 26.0 copy, 2026-08-12). Sibling libraries are not an edge case - Nils's own
       copy rule has a branch for them, and the pool model is built on them.

       Delegates to DestinationForRow on the file's FOLDER rather than restating the rule, so the
       two can never drift again. */
    Function DestinationForFile String sOldFile String sOldHome String sNewHome Returns String
        String sFolder sLeaf sDestFolder
        Integer iPos

        Move (RightPos("\", sOldFile)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Left(sOldFile, iPos)) to sFolder
        Move (Right(sOldFile, (Length(sOldFile) - iPos))) to sLeaf
        If (sLeaf = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        // The folder WITHOUT its trailing separator - the form _RelativeToHome compares against.
        Get RemoveFolderSeperator sFolder to sFolder
        Get DestinationForFolder sFolder sOldHome sNewHome to sDestFolder
        If (sDestFolder = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return (vFolderFormat(sDestFolder) + sLeaf)
    End_Function

    /* THE PLACEMENT RULE, on a plain path - where a folder that sat at sOldPath ends up in the copy.

       Lifted out of DestinationForRow (2026-08-12) so it can be asked about a path that is not a
       plan row. The rule was always about a PATH; taking a tCopyItem meant every other caller had to
       manufacture a struct to ask, and a struct passed to a same-object Get came back empty - which
       is how the first attempt at DestinationForFile silently returned "" for every case. One
       implementation, reachable by both. */
    Function DestinationForFolder String sOldPath String sOldHome String sNewHome Returns String
        String sRel sLeaf sOldParent sNewParent
        Integer iPos

        If (sOldPath = "" or sNewHome = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        /* THE DESTINATION MUST BE ABSOLUTE, and this is the one place that can guarantee it.

           A RELATIVE sNewHome does not fail - it succeeds somewhere else. Every branch below
           concatenates onto it, so "SpecializedComponents" produces "SpecializedComponents\AppSrc",
           which Windows resolves against the PROCESS working directory. On 2026-08-12 that scattered
           an entire workspace and its sibling library into
           C:\Users\rdcto\OneDrive\Documents\, with nothing at all reaching the E: drive the user
           chose - and no error, because writing to a valid relative path is not an error.

           It passed every guard upstream: not empty, not a drive root, not inside the source. None
           of them asks the only question that matters here. Refusing at the root means no caller can
           reintroduce it - the copy list comes back empty and the run reports that it wrote nothing,
           which is the correct outcome for a destination that was never usable. */
        If (_IsAbsolutePath(Self, sNewHome) = False) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get _RelativeToHome sOldPath sOldHome to sRel
        If (sRel <> "") Begin
            If (sRel = ".\") ;
                Function_Return (RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sNewHome))
            Function_Return (vFolderFormat(sNewHome) + Mid(sRel, Length(sRel), 3))
        End
        If (_MirrorsBesideHome(Self, sOldPath, sOldHome) = True) Begin
            Get _FolderOf (RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sOldHome)) to sOldParent
            Get _FolderOf (RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sNewHome)) to sNewParent
            If (sNewParent <> "") Begin
                Get _RelativeToHome sOldPath sOldParent to sRel
                Function_Return (vFolderFormat(sNewParent) + Mid(sRel, Length(sRel), 3))
            End
        End
        Move (RightPos("\", RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sOldPath))) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Right(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sOldPath), (Length(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sOldPath)) - iPos))) to sLeaf
        Function_Return (vFolderFormat(sNewHome) + sLeaf)
    End_Function

    // The plan-row form of the same question, kept because every existing caller asks it this way.
    Function DestinationForRow tCopyItem Item String sOldHome String sNewHome Returns String
        String sDest
        Get DestinationForFolder Item.sPath sOldHome sNewHome to sDest
        Function_Return sDest
    End_Function

    /* True when this row lands beside the new home in a folder that ALREADY EXISTS.

       That folder is a library pool the destination may share with OTHER workspaces, and this
       workspace's copy of a library is not necessarily the newest one in the world. Copying into
       it would overwrite their files with ours. So the copy uses what is already there and writes
       nothing - which is exactly what a shared pool is for, and leaves the .sws entry naming it
       still valid.

       The caller must also TELL the user, because the consequence is real: the copy then depends
       on a folder it does not own. ExistingSiblingFolders exists for that.

       Takes the row's path and its ALREADY-COMPUTED destination rather than the tCopyItem: a
       struct parameter forwarded to a further self-send arrives all-defaults, so the first cut of
       this read False for a pool that plainly existed - silently, with both its ingredients
       checking out under a probe (2026-08-06). The caller has the destination in hand anyway. */
    Function _UsesExistingSibling String sPath String sDest String sOldHome Returns Boolean
        If (sDest = "" or sPath = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        If (_MirrorsBesideHome(Self, sPath, sOldHome) = False) ;
            Function_Return False
        Function_Return (rfDirectoryExists(sDest))
    End_Function

    // The destination folders the copy will deliberately NOT write, because they already exist
    // beside the new home. Each entry is the destination path. Empty when nothing is reused.
    Function ExistingSiblingFolders tCopyItem[] aItems String sOldHome String sNewHome Returns String[]
        String[] asOut
        String sDest
        Boolean bReuse
        Integer i iMax

        Move (SizeOfArray(aItems) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aItems[i].bSelected = True and aItems[i].bExists = True and aItems[i].bIsLink = False) Begin
                Get DestinationForRow aItems[i] sOldHome sNewHome to sDest
                Get _UsesExistingSibling aItems[i].sPath sDest sOldHome to bReuse
                If (bReuse = True) ;
                    Move sDest to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    /* LIBRARIES DECLARED INSIDE THE HOME THAT THE COPY WILL NOT TAKE (Nils 2026-09-03, LegacyApp).

       VDFQuery and vWin32fh lived under the workspace's own Libraries\ folder and were unticked.
       Their [Libraries] entries went into the copy's .sws as written - the right rule for a
       library BESIDE the workspace, which the copy legitimately shares, and the wrong one here:
       inside the home there is nothing at that path in the copy. df-cli read the dangling pair,
       refused to convert the workspace, and Add Package stayed dim with no word about why.

       The workspace's OWN entries only (depth 0) - those are the lines NewSwsLinesDropping writes
       the copy's .sws from; a nested library's .sws is never rewritten from DF26 up. An entry
       counts when its .sws resolves INSIDE sOldHome and no chosen row's folder contains it. */
    Function UncopiedInsideHomeLibraries tCopyItem[] aChosen String sSwsFile String sOldHome Returns tLibraryInfo[]
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs aOut
        String sNoVersion sRel
        Integer i iMax
        Boolean bCovered

        // A variable, never an empty literal - see LibraryCopyItems.
        Move "" to sNoVersion
        Get WorkspaceLibraryInventory sSwsFile sNoVersion to aLibs
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Get _RelativeToHome aLibs[i].sRefSwsFile sOldHome to sRel
            If (sRel <> "") Begin
                Get _UnderAnyChosenRow aChosen aLibs[i].sFolder to bCovered
                If (bCovered = False) ;
                    Move aLibs[i] to aOut[SizeOfArray(aOut)]
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aOut
    End_Function

    // Is sFolder equal to, or inside, the path of one of the chosen rows?
    Function _UnderAnyChosenRow tCopyItem[] aItems String sFolder Returns Boolean
        String sLow sRow
        Integer i iMax

        Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, sFolder))) to sLow
        If (sLow = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (SizeOfArray(aItems) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, aItems[i].sPath))) to sRow
            If (sRow <> "" and (sLow = sRow or Left(sLow, Length(sRow) + 1) = (sRow + "\"))) ;
                Function_Return True
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* Every file the copy will write, source and destination, for the SELECTED rows only.

       Computed up front rather than copied folder-by-folder so the executor can report honest
       progress and stop between files - a 663 MB Data folder copied in one call is a minute of
       apparent hang with no way out.

       A row that is a LINK is skipped entirely: following a junction would duplicate whatever it
       points at (a shared library pool, typically) into the copy, and silently fork it.

       So is a row that lands in an EXISTING folder beside the new home - see
       _UsesExistingSibling. Skipping it here rather than in the copy loop keeps the file count,
       the byte total and the progress bar honest about what is actually going to be written.

       And so is every COMPILER OUTPUT file (_IsCompilerOutput: .prn / .err / .dep) - counted in
       piCompilerOutputsSkipped so the report can say so. */
    Function CopyFileList tCopyItem[] aItems String sOldHome String sNewHome Returns tCopyPair[]
        tCopyPair[] aOut
        tRFSearchResult[] asFiles
        String sDestRoot sSrcRoot sFile sRel
        Boolean bReuse
        Integer i iMax j jMax iRow iSkipped

        Move 0 to iSkipped
        Move (SizeOfArray(aItems) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aItems[i].bSelected = True and aItems[i].bExists = True and aItems[i].bIsLink = False) Begin
                Get DestinationForRow aItems[i] sOldHome sNewHome to sDestRoot
                Get _UsesExistingSibling aItems[i].sPath sDestRoot sOldHome to bReuse
                If (sDestRoot <> "" and bReuse = False) Begin
                    Move (RemoveFolderSeperator(Self, aItems[i].sPath)) to sSrcRoot
                    Move (rfFileSearchRecursive((sSrcRoot + "\*.*"), DIRMODE_FILES_ONLY, False)) to asFiles
                    Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles) - 1) to jMax
                    For j from 0 to jMax
                        // rfFileSearchRecursive returns FULL paths in sFilename.
                        Move asFiles[j].sFilename to sFile
                        If (Left(Lowercase(sFile), Length(sSrcRoot) + 1) = Lowercase(sSrcRoot + "\")) Begin
                            If (_IsCompilerOutput(Self, sFile)) Begin
                                Increment iSkipped
                            End
                            Else Begin
                                Move (Mid(sFile, Length(sFile), Length(sSrcRoot) + 2)) to sRel
                                Move (SizeOfArray(aOut)) to iRow
                                Move sFile to aOut[iRow].sSource
                                Move (vFolderFormat(sDestRoot) + sRel) to aOut[iRow].sDest
                            End
                        End
                    Loop
                End
            End
        Loop
        Set piCompilerOutputsSkipped to iSkipped
        Function_Return aOut
    End_Function

    /* The whole plan: declared paths, then libraries, then everything else under home.

       CHEAP - no recursive walks, so this is safe to call while building a tile. Row sizes are
       absent (bMeasured False) until MeasureCopyItem fills them in one row at a time. */
    Function BuildCopyPlan String sSwsFile Returns tCopyItem[]
        tCopyItem[] aItems aLibs aRest aDeclared
        Integer i iMax

        Get DeclaredCopyItems sSwsFile to aItems
        Get LibraryCopyItems sSwsFile to aLibs
        /* A LIBRARY ROW IS COVERED ONLY BY A DECLARED ROW, never by another library
           (2026-08-15, the WinServ copy that lost WebAppSrc itself while copying its
           children).

           _IsCovered treats a kept row NESTED UNDER the candidate as covering it - right for a
           declared path ("a more precise row already names something inside this folder"),
           and backwards for a library: a child library copied on its own does not carry the
           parent library's OWN files. The tree emits rows in [Libraries] order, and WinServ
           lists Lib2/Lib3 (vwin32fh, VDFQuery - both INSIDE WebAppSrc) before Lib1 (WebAppSrc),
           so by the time WebAppSrc was tested a child of it was already kept and it was
           judged covered - dropped, silently, while its children copied. df-cli then reported
           WebAppSrc-Library-DF191.sws missing at a path that was perfectly correct.

           So each library is tested against the DECLARED rows only, which is the coverage the
           rule was written for. Library-vs-library overlap is what LibraryCopyItems' one-row-
           per-root already handles, and a parent/child pair copies twice at worst - the child's
           files land in the same place under the parent, which is harmless and correct. */
        Get DeclaredCopyItems sSwsFile to aDeclared
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (_IsCovered(Self, aDeclared, aLibs[i].sPath) = False) ;
                Move aLibs[i] to aItems[SizeOfArray(aItems)]
        Loop

        Get UndeclaredCopyItems sSwsFile aItems to aRest
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRest) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move aRest[i] to aItems[SizeOfArray(aItems)]
        Loop

        Function_Return aItems
    End_Function

End_Class
